WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.995
Arrays represent contiguous elements, but C does not automatically preserve array length in every expression.

2
00:00:06.995 --> 00:00:12.296
When an array is passed to a function, it often decays to a pointer to its first element.

3
00:00:12.296 --> 00:00:18.629
The callee may receive an address without receiving the capacity information needed for safe access.

4
00:00:18.629 --> 00:00:27.611
Pointer arithmetic is defined only within the same array object, including the one-past-the-end position for comparison or loop termination.

5
00:00:27.611 --> 00:00:33.502
The one-past pointer is useful as a boundary marker, but it is not a valid object to dereference.

6
00:00:33.502 --> 00:00:39.540
Moving a pointer outside the array object loses the defined relationship the language provides.

7
00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:42.779
Element counts and byte counts must not be confused.

8
00:00:42.779 --> 00:00:48.228
A count of characters, integers, structure elements, or bytes means different things.

9
00:00:48.228 --> 00:00:56.696
Conversions between element counts and byte sizes should be reviewed for arithmetic overflow, object size, and destination capacity.

10
00:00:56.696 --> 00:01:03.838
Bounds checks should be based on the destination capacity, the current length, and the operation being performed.

11
00:01:03.838 --> 00:01:09.286
A copy, append, parse, transform, or null-termination step may need different checks.

12
00:01:09.286 --> 00:01:15.250
The fact that a source buffer is a certain size does not prove that the destination can receive it.

13
00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:20.846
Function contracts should carry capacity and length together when buffers are involved.

14
00:01:20.846 --> 00:01:30.124
They should also specify whether a pointer may be null, whether the buffer is mutable, whether the length includes a terminator, and who owns the data.

15
00:01:30.124 --> 00:01:34.174
Those details make array reasoning visible instead of implicit.
